Dear 3D Printing Industry Editorial Team,

Solidon3D 0.2.2 has been released. It is not an industrial PLM platform or a
slicer; it is local desktop software for the preparation gap between imported
geometry and an external polymer-AM slicing workflow.

The release adds three relevant capabilities:

* a recognised hole in imported STL or 3MF geometry can be resized while its
  original position and axis are preserved;
* each physical filament spool can carry temperatures, cooling, retraction and
  material data into the correct 3MF material slot and slicer handoff;
* reusable parametric components are assembled from registered operation
  history and values, without executable source in the project file.

Every geometry change remains a parameterised, reproducible operation. An
optional language model may invoke the same registered API as the UI, but it
cannot calculate geometry or execute generated source. Editing, analysis and
export remain available without AI, an account or an internet connection.

Since the first public 0.1 demo, the release history records 241 curated
user-facing changes; 0.2.2 itself accounts for 40. Solidon is packaged for
Windows, macOS and Linux and supports a file-based activation route for
workstations that remain offline.

Product: https://solidon3d.de/en/
Release history: https://solidon3d.de/en/changelog.html

I can provide the released build, a technical fact sheet, a sample STL-to-3MF
workflow and the resulting slicer handoff data for editorial evaluation.

